INT. SIGNAL RELAY HUT - HIMALAYAN PASS - DAY

Stone walls. Tin roof ticking in the wind. A hand-crank radio on a plank table. Frost on the window. 1962.

SGT. HAKIM BARAKZAI, 40, beard gone grey at the chin, sits on an ammo crate. Mending a boot. Slow.

PVT. LUCJAN NOWAK, 19, shivering, wrapped in two blankets, stares at the stove. Feeds it one stick at a time.

NOWAK
Last of the wood.

BARAKZAI
Burn the crate after.

NOWAK
Which crate?

BARAKZAI
The one you're not sitting on.

Static. A crackle from the radio.

COMMAND (V.O.)
(garbled)
Relay Nine, Relay Nine. Report status. Over.

Barakzai crosses and takes the handset.

BARAKZAI
Command, Relay Nine. Two men. One stove. No contact. Over.

COMMAND (V.O.)
Copy two men. Supply drop postponed. Weather. Over.

BARAKZAI
Understood. Out.

He hangs up. Nowak keeps his eyes on the flames.

NOWAK
That's the third postponement.

BARAKZAI
Fourth. You weren't counting.

The door BANGS open. Wind and snow. A figure in a white parka stumbles in, shoulders a medical satchel: MEDIC ANNELI SAARI, 30, Finnish, windburned face, ski goggles pushed up. She slams the door behind her.

SAARI
Which of you is Barakzai?

BARAKZAI
Me.

SAARI
Good. I walked nine hours. Is there tea?

NOWAK
No.

SAARI
Is there anything?

BARAKZAI
Boiled snow.

SAARI
(dropping her satchel)
Then boil it.

Nowak scrambles for the kettle. Saari sits heavily next to Barakzai and pulls off a glove, examining her blue fingertips.

BARAKZAI
Command said no supply.

SAARI
Command said nothing to me. I was sent for the private's feet.

NOWAK
My feet are fine.

SAARI
Take your boots off.

NOWAK
They're fine.

BARAKZAI
Take them off, Lucjan.

Reluctantly, Nowak peels off a boot and a grey sock. The toes are white. Saari looks, then at Barakzai.

SAARI
Fine. For now.

COMMAND (V.O.)
(over radio)
Relay Nine, be advised, weather window tomorrow oh-six-hundred. Over.

Barakzai doesn't move to answer. He looks at the kettle, at the stove, at the two young faces.

BARAKZAI
Tomorrow, then.
